WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.871
A payment service wires itself by hand, in main.

2
00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:12.539
Each object is built after the ones its constructor needs: the config first, the payment service last.

3
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:16.326
A dependency injection container does that wiring for you.

4
00:00:16.326 --> 00:00:18.741
Here is the one you'll build, finished.

5
00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:26.166
At start-up it finds the components, picks their constructors, orders the graph, and builds each singleton once.

6
00:00:26.166 --> 00:00:28.491
Timed and Retry methods get a proxy.

7
00:00:28.491 --> 00:00:33.140
A generator can write the same wiring at build time, for a faster start.

8
00:00:33.467 --> 00:00:37.292
Lesson 1, in Java, wires classes by reflection.

9
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:42.913
The scanner loads each class without initializing it, so no static initializer runs.

10
00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:47.748
It never guesses a constructor, and it skips the synthetic ones Kotlin adds.

11
00:00:47.933 --> 00:00:52.335
A depth-first walk orders the graph, and names any cycle at start-up.

12
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:59.199
Each singleton gets a cell with double-checked locking: one instance, even when eight threads ask at once.

13
00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.756
Lesson 2, in Kotlin, adds Timed and Retry.

14
00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:09.404
Interceptors run around each call, with Timed outside Retry, so the time covers every attempt.

15
00:01:09.667 --> 00:01:14.537
A component with an interface gets a JDK proxy around its target.

16
00:01:14.733 --> 00:01:17.852
A class without one gets a Byte Buddy subclass.

17
00:01:17.852 --> 00:01:24.091
A subclass can't override what Kotlin leaves final, so the container refuses that at start-up.

18
00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:28.028
Lesson 3, in Java, measures it and makes it start fast.

19
00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:35.780
Per call, JMH counts 208 bytes for a JDK proxy, and 160 for a subclass.

20
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:40.039
At start-up, the app loads 2,207 classes.

21
00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:44.732
A class histogram and a heap dump show what the container keeps alive.

22
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:51.661
Then a generator runs the same graph at build time, and writes a holder class per singleton.

23
00:01:51.661 --> 00:01:53.928
The start drops to 991 classes.

24
00:01:54.133 --> 00:01:55.244
It writes Java.

25
00:01:55.244 --> 00:01:58.797
In Kotlin, an object is the same kind of holder.

26
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:03.212
AppCDS and the AOT cache then archive what is left.

27
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:07.523
One rule holds throughout: never guess, and never skip in silence.

28
00:02:07.523 --> 00:02:14.144
What the classes alone can tell, the container checks before the first call, at start-up, or in the build.
